  "text": "We measured how the polarization of light scattered in an inelastic process changes for the fully symmetric vibrational mode of a common carbonate crystal, occurring near a frequency of one thousand reciprocal centimeters. The experiment used a tightly collimated gas‑based laser beam to excite the sample. The observed depolarization ratios matched the values predicted by symmetry‑based polarization rules and showed no sign of the unusually high ratios reported in earlier work. The study also explains how apparent changes in depolarization can arise when the incident light is not perfectly parallel but instead converges, causing interference between different polarization components. These interference effects are examined in detail, confirming that the true scattering behavior follows the standard theoretical expectations."
